.TH CHTTPSVR_ENGINE_STOP 3 "2026-07-14" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_engine_stop \- signal the engine to stop
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

void chttpsvr_engine_stop(void);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Tells the shared reactor of chttpserver, and the idle-timeout sweep
thread beside it, to stop, and forces them down even while other
chttpsvr instances in the same process hold a reference and are fully
started.  This reactor is separate from the engine of chttpclient, so
this call has no effect on the async work of chttpclient in the same
process (chttpclient_do_async, chttpclient_do_async_streaming,
chttpclient_do_pooled and chttpclient_do_pooled_streaming).  It
returns at once; use chttpsvr_engine_wait() to block until the stop
completes.

Before it tears the shared reactor down, the library quiesces every
chttpsvr instance that is registered in the process, started or not,
in exactly the way chttpsvr_destroy() does: it closes the listener,
drains the in-flight requests, closes the idle and parked connections
and the requests that wait for body memory, answers the queued
streaming requests with 503, shuts the worker pools down and frees the
engine reference of that instance.
Every registered instance stops taking new requests as soon as the
stop begins, before the instances are quiesced one after another.
Idle keep-alive connections are closed, a request that arrives later
gets 503 Service Unavailable with Connection: close and runs no
handler, and the response of a request that is already running
carries Connection: close.  So the drain ends when the running
handlers return; see chttpsvr_destroy(3).
Because of this quiescing, the ONE chttpsvr_destroy(srv) call in the
signal-handler shutdown pattern below is safe, even though it runs
after the library has torn the engine down: srv is quiesced by then,
so that call only frees the remaining memory of the server and
repeats no teardown work.  Repeated destroy calls on the same handle
are not made safe by this: a second chttpsvr_destroy(srv) on a handle
that an earlier call destroyed is a fatal error, with abort() and
SIGABRT, on this path as on every other one.

    static void on_signal(int sig) { (void)sig; chttpsvr_engine_stop(); }
    /* ... */
    if (chttpsvr_start(srv, &cfg) != ccol_success) {
        chttpsvr_destroy(srv);
        chttpsvr_engine_wait();   /* the stop can be incomplete */
        return 1;
    }
    chttpsvr_engine_wait();   /* returns once on_signal fires */
    chttpsvr_destroy(srv);    /* safe: srv was already quiesced above */

This function does not block and is async-signal-safe, so you can
call it directly from a POSIX signal handler, for SIGINT or SIGTERM
for example.  It is the only function of this module that is
documented as async-signal-safe.  chttpsvr_stop() is not (see the
WARNINGS section of that function), so do not call chttpsvr_stop()
directly from a signal handler.

The library installs no signal handlers, so an application has to
connect this function to whatever signal or shutdown mechanism it
uses.

A call stops only the engine that runs at the moment of the call.
Calling this function before the reactor has ever started, or while
no engine runs, is safe and does nothing.  If the engine of a call
has stopped by the time the library acts on the request, the call
never stops an engine that a later chttpsvr_start brought up.

A call that lands while chttpsvr_destroy() is draining a server waits
for that drain: chttpsvr_engine_wait() returns only after the
handlers of that server have returned.

You can call it more than once, also while the drain that an earlier
call started is running, which is what happens when two SIGTERMs
arrive moments apart or when the shutdown code of an application
makes an extra defensive call.  A repeated or overlapping call does
nothing and never starts a second teardown.
.fi
